ions it started.\n\n### New\n\n- **Sessions**, under each account in the menu: that account's Code-tab sessions, newest first, read from the records Claude Desktop keeps for it. Read-only.\n- **Copy to <account>…**, on each session. It makes an *independent* copy for the other account: the conversation as it is right now, under a new id, filed where that account's Claude keeps its sessions. The two accounts never share a file, and later messages in one are not added to the other.\n  - The copy is listed **the next time that account's Claude starts**. Claude Switcher will not quit Claude for you.\n  - It only creates files. It never modifies or removes anything of Claude's, never writes into the account it copies from, and follows no symbolic link on the way. If it is interrupted, the next start finishes the copy or cleans up after it and says which.\n  - Not copyable, with the reason shown on the row: a session still replying, in a git worktree or scratch workspace, spanning several transcripts, watching an artifact's comments, remote or archived; and a target that is signed out, signed in to a different account, or in more than one organisation.\n  - Chat-tab conversations cannot be copied: they live with each account on claude.ai.\n\n  **This is new, and it has not yet been run against a live Claude.** It was built from Claude Desktop 2.19675.0's own program code — it is a port of Claude's same-account fork — and tested in temporary directories (480 tests). What only a real run can show has not been shown: that the copy is listed after the restart, that the first reply works under the other account, and which permission mode the copy starts in. Try it first on a session you could do without. The README's \"The first live copy\" is a five-step check, with what each outcome means.\n- **A welcome window**: on first launch, whenever Claude Switcher is opened again while it is already running, and from **Welcome…** in the menu. It says where the icon is and what an account is, and its **Show Menu** button opens the menu from the window — for when macOS is hiding the icon (a full menu bar, or on macOS 26, System Settings › Menu Bar › Allow in the Menu Bar).\n- **Selecting a running account brings its window back**, even if it was closed. Claude keeps running with its main window closed, and activating it alone raised nothing. The switcher now also sends that one Claude the same \"reopen\" request a Dock click sends. **Diagnostics…** shows whether macOS let the last one through; if it did not, the account is activated as before.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **\"Profile\" is \"account\"** everywhere on screen. `config.json` is unchanged — its keys still say `profiles` — so there is nothing to migrate.\n- The terminal sign-in badge moved off the account rows into **Copy terminal command**, as \"signed in\" or \"no sign-in found\". It only ever described the `claude` CLI, not the app.\n- The \"Claude CLI not found\" alert at launch is now a line in that submenu.\n- Nothing that saves runs while `config.json` cannot be read. Adding, renaming or removing an account in that state would have written the defaults over the file.\n- README: the section on the Code tab's session list is corrected, and there is a full account of what copying a session creates, in what order, and what has and has not been verified.\n\n### Install\n\nDownload **Claude Switcher.dmg**, open it, and drag the app to Applications, replacing the old one. Signed and notarized. Quit the running Claude Switcher from its menu first, then open the new one.\n\nDetails in the README: \"Copying a session to another account\" and \"Launched it and nothing happened?\".\n\nNot affiliated with or endorsed by Anthropic.\n"}
